menu "Firefly BLE Proxy"

    comment "ESP-NOW must match the panels' settings exactly"

    config FIREFLY_ESPNOW_CHANNEL
        int "ESP-NOW WiFi channel"
        default 1
        range 1 13
        help
            MUST match the channel every panel is built with — there is no
            AP to negotiate one, so a mismatch means the broadcasts are
            silently invisible. The panels default to channel 1.

    config FIREFLY_ESPNOW_PEER_MAC
        string "ESP-NOW unicast peer MAC (unused by this node)"
        default "AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF"
        help
            Not used. This node runs the telemetry-only role: it broadcasts
            and never unicasts, so it has no peer. The symbol exists because
            the shared espnow_link component references it. Leave it at the
            placeholder.

    config FIREFLY_ESPNOW_PMK
        string "ESP-NOW primary master key (16 ASCII chars)"
        default "firefly-pmk-0001"
        help
            Only used for encrypted unicast peers, which this node has none
            of — broadcast frames cannot be encrypted at all. Kept so the
            shared component compiles; harmless to leave at the default.

    config FIREFLY_ESPNOW_LMK
        string "ESP-NOW peer local master key (16 ASCII chars)"
        default "firefly-lmk-0001"
        help
            As above — unused by the telemetry-only role.

    config FIREFLY_ESPNOW_VALVE_PEER_MAC
        string "ESP-NOW valve peer MAC (unused by this node)"
        default "AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF"
        help
            Not used. This is mid_coach's SECOND ESP-NOW peer (the valve
            node) — irrelevant to a telemetry-only broadcaster. The symbol
            exists because espnow_link.c references it unconditionally.
            Leave it at the placeholder.

    config FIREFLY_ESPNOW_VALVE_LMK
        string "ESP-NOW valve peer local master key (16 ASCII chars)"
        default "firefly-lmk-0001"
        help
            As above — unused by the telemetry-only role.

    config FIREFLY_TELEMETRY_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Telemetry broadcast interval (ms)"
        default 5000
        range 1000 60000
        help
            How often the latest Power Watchdog reading is broadcast to the
            panels.

            Note this is NOT a poll interval: the Watchdog streams
            notifications about once a second on its own and offers no way
            to slow that down. This only controls how often we re-broadcast
            the newest values, which is also what makes a dropped broadcast
            self-healing — the next one corrects it.

    config FIREFLY_BATTERY_ENABLED
        bool "Connect to the JBD/Xiaoxiang battery packs"
        default y
        help
            Hold a BLE link to each configured battery pack and broadcast
            per-pack volts/amps/SOC/capacity/temperature.

            The packs are in the same basement bay as this node, which is
            the whole reason the links live here rather than on a wall
            panel: no steel bay door in the RF path, and the panel's radio
            is left to ESP-NOW.

            Panels combine the per-pack broadcasts themselves
            (jbd_bms_combine) rather than receiving a pre-summed bank, so
            the aggregation stays in one host-tested place and the panel's
            per-pack detail popup still has real numbers to show.

    config FIREFLY_BATTERY_1_MAC
        string "Battery 1 BLE MAC address (AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF)"
        depends on FIREFLY_BATTERY_ENABLED
        default "00:00:00:00:00:00"
        help
            BLE MAC of the first Xiaoxiang/JBD BMS. The placeholder
            all-zero address means "unconfigured" -- that slot is skipped
            entirely (no connection attempted, nothing broadcast) until a
            real MAC is set.

            Slot order matters: it is the slot index that goes out on the
            wire, and it is what a panel labels its detail-popup rows by.

    config FIREFLY_BATTERY_2_MAC
        string "Battery 2 BLE MAC address (AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF)"
        depends on FIREFLY_BATTERY_ENABLED
        default "00:00:00:00:00:00"
        help
            Same as FIREFLY_BATTERY_1_MAC, for the second battery.

    config FIREFLY_BATTERY_3_MAC
        string "Battery 3 BLE MAC address (AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF)"
        depends on FIREFLY_BATTERY_ENABLED
        default "00:00:00:00:00:00"
        help
            Same as FIREFLY_BATTERY_1_MAC, for the third battery.

    config FIREFLY_BATTERY_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Battery poll / broadcast interval (ms)"
        default 30000
        range 20000 600000
        help
            How often each connected battery is asked for a fresh
            basic-info reading, and therefore how often its values are
            re-broadcast to the panels.

            The 20 s lower bound is deliberate, not arbitrary: JBD BMS
            units are widely reported to misbehave when polled more often
            than roughly every 20 seconds, so the floor is enforced here
            rather than merely documented.

            Note jbd_bms_client.c derives its own "battery is healthy"
            staleness window from this value (3x), and the panels derive
            their "pack has gone quiet" window the same way -- so keep this
            in step with the panels' setting.

    config FIREFLY_WD_ENABLED
        bool "Connect to a Hughes Power Watchdog"
        default y
        help
            Scan for and subscribe to a Gen 1 (Bluetooth-only) Power
            Watchdog, and broadcast its per-line volts/amps/watts.

    config FIREFLY_SOLAR_ENABLED
        bool "Connect to a Renogy MPPT charge controller"
        default y
        help
            Hold a BLE link to a Renogy solar charge controller through its
            BT-1/BT-2 module and broadcast battery volts, PV current and
            power, and temperatures.

            The controller has no radio of its own: the BT-2 is a
            transparent BLE-to-RS485 bridge, so this speaks plain Modbus RTU
            rather than a vendor frame format. See
            components/renogy_solar/include/renogy_solar_protocol.h.

    config FIREFLY_SOLAR_NAME
        string "Renogy module BLE advertised name"
        depends on FIREFLY_SOLAR_ENABLED
        default "BT-TH-B00E7B91"
        help
            Exact advertised name of the BT-1/BT-2 module, e.g.
            BT-TH-B00E7B91. Enter it as printed on the device -- trailing
            whitespace is ignored when matching, because the module pads its
            advertised name to a fixed width (this coach's sends
            "BT-TH-B00E7B91    ", four trailing spaces).

            Matching the exact name rather than the generic "BT-TH-" prefix
            matters in a campground: a neighbouring rig's Renogy module
            advertises the same prefix, and connecting to it would produce a
            perfectly healthy-looking link reporting somebody else's solar.

            Leave EMPTY to fall back to matching any known Renogy prefix --
            useful for a first bring-up when the name is not known yet, since
            the client logs whatever it finds.

    config FIREFLY_SOLAR_MAC
        string "Renogy module BLE MAC (optional override)"
        depends on FIREFLY_SOLAR_ENABLED
        default "00:00:00:00:00:00"
        help
            Optional. Set a real address to connect directly and skip
            discovery entirely, the way the battery packs do.

            The all-zero placeholder means "not set": discover by name
            instead. Worth pinning once the address is known from the log --
            it removes the scan, and with it the only part of bring-up that
            depends on the module happening to advertise at the right moment.

    config FIREFLY_SOLAR_DEVICE_ID
        int "Modbus device id of the charge controller"
        depends on FIREFLY_SOLAR_ENABLED
        default 255
        range 1 255
        help
            The RS485 address the controller answers on behind the BT-2.
            255 is the broadcast address and is what a stand-alone
            controller responds to; daisy-chained wiring uses 16/17 and
            Communication-Hub wiring 96/97.

            A wrong value produces SILENCE, not an error -- so the client
            does not simply sit there: after a couple of unanswered polls it
            moves to the next known candidate and logs the one that finally
            answers. Set this to that value afterwards to skip the probing on
            every later boot.

    config FIREFLY_SOLAR_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Solar poll / broadcast interval (ms)"
        depends on FIREFLY_SOLAR_ENABLED
        default 30000
        range 5000 600000
        help
            How often the controller is asked for a fresh reading, and
            therefore how often its values are re-broadcast.

            No JBD-style vendor limitation applies here -- Modbus polling is
            what these controllers are built for -- but there is nothing to
            gain from polling a charge controller faster than the batteries:
            it shares the radio with four other BLE links plus ESP-NOW.

            Note renogy_solar_client.c DERIVES its staleness window from this
            value (3x) rather than hardcoding one. Hardcoding it is what made
            every battery pack read as permanently offline when the JBD poll
            interval moved to 30 s.

endmenu
